Design-for-testing (DfT) is a methodology in which the testing requirements are considered during the design phase of product development. The goal of DFT is to ensure that products can be easily and effectively tested during remanufacturing, so that any defects can be quickly identified and corrected. 

By incorporating DfT into the design process, manufacturers can improve the quality of their products and reduce the cost of remanufacturing. 

DfT includes both hardware and software elements. Hardware design for testing includes the development of test fixtures, test probes, and test equipment. Software design for testing includes the development of test programs, test data, and test cases.

New technologies are improving design for testing in the context of remanufacturing in several ways. For example, new sensor technologies can be used to more accurately detect defects in products, and new data analysis techniques can be used to more effectively identify trends in manufacturing processes. Additionally, new inspection technologies can be used to more accurately assess the condition of products and new test equipment can be used to more effectively test products for defects.